1. PURPOSE AND WORKFLOW SCOPE
This standard operating procedure delineates the step-by-step administrative procedures governing initial bus pass enrollment, route adjustments, pickup stop reassignments, pass cancellations, and prorated fee refunds for Demo University commuter transit. Enforced by DEPT-TRANS in coordination with Finance and Accounts (DEPT-FIN) and Security (DEPT-SEC).

2. INITIAL BUS PASS ENROLLMENT WORKFLOW (REL-016)
Students seeking commuter bus services must execute the following four-step registration protocol:
2.1 Step 1 (Portal Selection): Log in to the TransPass transit portal at https://transpass.demo.edu.internal using UniCampus ERP credentials. Navigate to 'New Bus Pass Application';
2.2 Step 2 (Route and Stop Selection): Review the interactive route timetable; select the appropriate Route Number (Routes 1–12) and the exact morning/evening pickup stop nearest to the student's verified residence;
2.3 Step 3 (Fee Payment): The system automatically computes the zonal fee. The student completes online payment through PayVarsity (SYS-PAY) via UPI, net banking, or debit card. An authenticated digital receipt is generated immediately;
2.4 Step 4 (RFID Sync and SLA): Upon payment confirmation, TransPass transmits authorized transit credentials to CampusPass within twenty-four (24) working hours. The student's physical Smart RFID ID Card is activated automatically for handheld scanner tap-in on the selected route.

3. ROUTE AND PICKUP STOP MODIFICATION WORKFLOW
Students relocating their residence mid-semester may request a route or stop adjustment:
3.1 Eligibility: Route change requests are accepted only during the first ten (10) working days of each semester, or upon submission of an authenticated proof of residential address change (e.g., parental rental agreement);
3.2 Application on TransPass: The student submits a 'Route Change Request' on TransPass specifying the current route, proposed new route, and requested pickup node;
3.3 Differential Fee Adjustment: If transitioning to a higher fee zone, the difference must be cleared on PayVarsity within 48 hours; if moving to a lower fee zone, no mid-term differential refund is issued;
3.4 Processing SLA: The updated route privilege is reprogrammed onto the RFID card within two (2) working days of supervisor sign-off.

4. PASS CANCELLATION AND FEE REFUND NORMS (FIN-POL-004)
If a student relocates into campus residential hostels or withdraws from the university, bus pass refunds are processed under the following statutory refund tiers:
4.1 Cancellation Prior to Class Commencement: Ninety percent (90%) refund of the assessed annual transport fee, with 10% retained as an administrative processing surcharge;
4.2 Cancellation Within First 15 Calendar Days of Semester: Fifty percent (50%) refund of the assessed semester fee;
4.3 Cancellation After 15 Calendar Days: Zero refund, except in cases of certified medical leaves of absence sanctioned by the Dean of Academic Affairs or formal institutional withdrawal (POL-TRANSFER-CERT);
4.4 Refund Credit: Approved refunds are credited directly to the student's verified bank account registered in PayVarsity within fourteen (14) working days.

1. PURPOSE AND INSTITUTIONAL MANDATE
This policy establishes standardized operational workflows, preventive inspection schedules, priority categorizations, and service-level agreements (SLAs) governing the physical upkeep, civil maintenance, electrical grid management, plumbing, and structural safety across all academic buildings, student activity zones, and auxiliary facilities of Demo University. Enforced by Campus Operations, Estate and Facilities Management (DEPT-OPS), headquartered in the Central Utility Building.

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (CO)
Within university administrative nomenclature, the abbreviation 'CO' denotes two completely separate entities:
2.1 Campus Operations (CO under DEPT-OPS / OPS-POL-001): The operational estate management department responsible for civil buildings, air-conditioning, electrical substations, water purification plants, classroom furniture, and facility repairs;
2.2 Course Outcome (CO under DEPT-ACAD / ACAD-OBE-001): The measurable academic competency and skill benchmark mapped to syllabus units within the Outcome-Based Education (OBE) and NBA accreditation framework.
- Query Disambiguation Rule: Inquiries regarding physical repairs, painting, broken desks, water leaks, or electrical issues refer strictly to Campus Operations (OPS-POL-001); inquiries regarding syllabus outcomes, Bloom's taxonomy levels, or exam mapping refer to Course Outcomes (ACAD-OBE-001).

3. SERVICE REQUEST LODGING VIA RESOLVEDESK
All facility defects, breakdowns, and repair requisitions must be formally registered on ResolveDesk (SYS-RESOLVE) at https://resolvedesk.demo.edu.internal under Category: Campus Operations:
3.1 Civil Maintenance: Broken floor tiles, masonry cracks, window pane replacements, door hardware, carpentry, and classroom desks;
3.2 Plumbing and Sanitation: Restroom leaks, clogged drainage, overhead tank overflows, and drinking water dispenser filter replacements;
3.3 Electrical Infrastructure: Power point failures, classroom lighting, ceiling fans, circuit breaker trips, and localized UPS backup faults;
3.4 Housekeeping and Grounds: Waste collection, common area sanitization, tree pruning, and estate pest control.

4. PRIORITY CLASSIFICATIONS AND SERVICE-LEVEL AGREEMENTS (SLAS)
Maintenance requisitions are triaged into three response tiers:
4.1 Tier 1 (Emergency Priority - 4-Hour Response SLA): Major electrical short circuits, burst primary water mains, sewer blockages, or hazardous structural ceiling collapses. Immediate dispatch of on-duty emergency technicians;
4.2 Tier 2 (Urgent Priority - 24 to 48-Hour Response SLA): Classroom projection power faults, air-conditioning failure in active lecture halls, broken entrance door locks, or restroom fixture leaks;
4.3 Tier 3 (Routine Priority - 5 Working Days SLA): Aesthetic touch-up painting, general carpentry adjustments, planned furniture reallocations, and routine landscaping.

5. PREVENTIVE AUDITS AND ESCALATION PATHWAYS
Preventive maintenance schedules (electrical substation overhauls, generator test runs, water tank chemical sterilizations) are executed during semester inter-sessions. Tickets exceeding statutory SLA windows automatically escalate to the Estate Site Engineer (OPS-EST-004) and Director of Campus Operations.

1. PURPOSE AND SPACE INVENTORY
This policy establishes reservation criteria, allocation hierarchies, technological provisions, cancelation timelines, and decorum standards governing institutional conference rooms, departmental seminar theaters, and major performance auditoriums administered by the Classroom Allocation Desk of Campus Operations (DEPT-OPS).

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (CR)
Within university administrative taxonomy, the acronym 'CR' possesses three distinct, non-overlapping meanings:
2.1 Conference Room (CR under DEPT-OPS / OPS-FAC-003): The bookable institutional venue designed for committee meetings, guest symposiums, and administrative deliberations (e.g., CR-1, CR-2 in Turing Complex, Ramanujan Executive Hall);
2.2 Class Representative (CR under DEPT-DSA / STU-LDR-001): The elected student representative serving as cohort liaison between students, course instructors, and academic departments;
2.3 Course Registration (CR under DEPT-ACAD / ACAD-POL-001): The statutory academic process of enrolling in semester courses and electives on UniCampus ERP.
- Cross-Domain Disambiguation Note: General institutional Conference Rooms (OPS-FAC-003) must not be confused with Library Discussion Cubicles (LIB-POL-003), which are bookable strictly for student study groups through LibNet.

3. CAMPUS VENUE INVENTORY AND AMENITIES
3.1 Conference Rooms (CR-1 to CR-6): Seating capacities of 20 to 50 persons, equipped with motorized projection screens, executive teleconference cameras, localized air conditioning (OPS-FAC-005), and interactive smartboards;
3.2 Departmental Seminar Halls: Capacities of 100 to 150 tiered seats located in Ramanujan and Turing complexes, equipped with dual projection systems and wireless lapel microphones;
3.3 Newton Central Auditorium: Premier 800-seat performance venue equipped with line-array acoustics, stage theatrical lighting, backstage green rooms, and dedicated 3-phase generator power.

4. RESERVATION PROCEDURES AND TIMELINES (REL-008)
4.1 Faculty and Academic Requisitions: Department Heads and faculty organizers must submit venue requisitions on ResolveDesk at least three (3) working days in advance;
4.2 Student Club and Society Requisitions: Recognized student clubs must submit venue requests accompanied by the formal SAC Event Sanction letter (SAC-POL-001) at least ten (10) working days prior to the event;
4.3 Priority Allocation Hierarchy: If scheduling conflicts emerge, space is allocated strictly under the following statutory hierarchy:
- Statutory Apex Meetings (Executive Council, Academic Council, Board of Governors);
- Centralized Examinations and University Placement Drives (DEPT-CDC / DEPT-EXAM);
- Official University Conferences, Symposia, and Accreditation Audits;
- Recognized Student Club Co-Curricular Competitions and Fests.

5. USAGE NORMS, CLEANLINESS, AND DAMAGE RESTITUTION
5.1 Consumables Restriction: Consuming meals, open beverages, or snacks inside auditoriums and conference rooms is strictly prohibited; refreshments must be served in designated outdoor foyers or dining courtyards;
5.2 Audiovisual Handover: Authorized AV technicians conduct pre-event and post-event equipment diagnostics. Organizers are held financially responsible under POL-DISCIPLINE for any physical damage to projection optics, acoustic microphones, or stage equipment caused by negligence.

1. PURPOSE AND SUSTAINABILITY COMMITMENT
This policy establishes statutory environmental setpoints, operating schedules, preventive service routines, and electrical conservation mandates governing central heating, ventilation, and air-conditioning (HVAC) plant systems, variable refrigerant flow (VRF) units, and split air-conditioning equipment across all campuses of Demo University. Enforced by the Electrical & HVAC Operations Unit of DEPT-OPS.

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (AC)
Within Demo University administrative documentation, the abbreviation 'AC' designates two completely different institutional concepts:
2.1 Air Conditioning (AC under DEPT-OPS / OPS-FAC-005): The mechanical thermal comfort infrastructure, split units, ventilation blowers, and central chillers regulating building climate;
2.2 Academic Council (AC under DEPT-ACAD / GOV-REG-002): The apex statutory academic body of the university approving all degree curricula, course regulations, examination criteria, and academic policies.
- Disambiguation Rule: Text referencing temperature setpoints, thermostats, cooling ducts, compressors, or power bills refers to Air Conditioning (OPS-FAC-005); text referencing degree approvals, academic regulations, syllabus reforms, or standing committees refers to Academic Council (GOV-REG-002).

3. MANDATORY STATUTORY TEMPERATURE SETPOINTS
In compliance with national energy efficiency directives and the university's environmental sustainability charter:
3.1 Standard Temperature Band: Thermostats across all administrative offices, staff rooms, conference halls, and teaching lecture theaters must be maintained strictly between twenty-four degrees Celsius (24°C) and twenty-six degrees Celsius (26°C);
3.2 Prohibition of Overcooling: Tampering with thermostat lockboxes or lowering room temperatures below 22°C is strictly forbidden;
3.3 Doors and Windows Mandate: Exterior doors, corridor windows, and ventilation louvers must remain fully closed while air-conditioning systems are engaged to prevent thermal loss and compressor overload.

4. OPERATING SCHEDULES ACROSS CAMPUS ZONES
4.1 Academic and Administrative Blocks: Central HVAC chillers operate on scheduled lecture days from 08:30 AM to 05:30 PM. Units are shut down automatically overnight and on weekends, except for authorized special events (OPS-FAC-003);
4.2 Continuous Mission-Critical Cooling: Data center server vaults (Turing Complex Server Room IT-SYS-001), high-performance computing clusters, and Central Library 24/7 digital hub maintain 24/7 dedicated precision air-conditioning with redundant backup chillers;
4.3 Residential Hostels (Nilgiri AC Wing): Air-conditioning operates from 08:00 PM to 07:00 AM on weekdays and 01:00 PM to 05:00 PM on weekends. Enrolled residents pay authorized supplemental utility electricity fees via PayVarsity under HSTL-REG-001.

5. PROHIBITED APPLIANCES AND BREAKDOWN REPORTING
5.1 Prohibited Private Appliances: In accordance with HSTL-POL-003, private high-power electrical appliances—including portable space heaters, immersion water rods, and personal air coolers—are strictly banned in student dormitories due to electrical fire hazard;
5.2 Maintenance Reporting: Compressor failures, unusual vibrating noises, water drippage, or refrigerant leaks must be logged immediately on ResolveDesk under Category: Campus Operations > HVAC for resolution within 24 hours.

1. PURPOSE AND PROFESSIONAL CHARTER
This document establishes the statutory operational duties, routine inspection frequencies, quality-assurance guidelines, structural safety audit protocols, and emergency response responsibilities for Estate Site Engineers appointed within Campus Operations, Estate and Facilities Management (DEPT-OPS).

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (SE)
Within university administrative nomenclature, the abbreviation 'SE' denotes three distinct entities across engineering, examinations, and facilities:
2.1 Site Engineer (SE under DEPT-OPS / OPS-EST-004): The full-time civil engineering professional responsible for structural safety, estate maintenance, contractor supervision, and physical building inspections;
2.2 Software Engineering (SE under DEPT-CSE / ACAD-CRS-005): The undergraduate computer science course (CSE305) covering agile development methodologies and software lifecycle design;
2.3 Semester Exam (SE under DEPT-EXAM / EXAM-GUI-002): The informal colloquial term used by students when querying Semester End Examinations (SEE).
- Disambiguation Instruction: Searches mentioning structural repairs, ceiling cracks, roofing leaks, carpentry, concrete testing, or contractor oversight point exclusively to the Site Engineer (OPS-EST-004).

3. ROUTINE CIVIL INFRASTRUCTURE INSPECTION DUTIES
Estate Site Engineers execute structured daily, weekly, and monthly surveillance routines across the physical campus estate:
3.1 Structural Integrity Monitoring: Inspecting foundations, load-bearing pillars, beams, expansion joints, and external brick masonry across Ramanujan Block, Chanakya Bhavan, Turing Complex, and Nilgiri Hostels for hairline cracks, water seepage, or structural fatigue;
3.2 Roofing, Parapets, and Drainage: Conducting monthly pre-monsoon and post-monsoon audits of rooftop waterproofing membranes, rainwater drainage spouts, overhead water reservoir supports, and parapet railings;
3.3 Fire Stairwells and Emergency Egress: Inspecting all external fire escape stairways, safety handrails, illuminated exit signage, and fire door hardware weekly to guarantee unhindered egress under SEC-SOP-001;
3.4 Perimeter Security Enclosures: Auditing perimeter boundary walls, razor-wire security toppings, vehicle crash barriers, and stormwater culverts in coordination with Campus Security (DEPT-SEC).

4. CONTRACTOR SUPERVISION AND QUALITY AUDITING
4.1 Quality Compliance: Supervising civil, plumbing, painting, and carpentry contractors engaged in university repair or capital expansion projects; ensuring raw materials conform to national civil engineering codes;
4.2 Measurement and Billing Verification: Reviewing contractor measurement sheets, conducting destructive/non-destructive material tests, and validating completion milestones before recommending fiscal sign-off to the Director of Campus Operations.

5. EMERGENCY RESPONSE AND HAZARD REMEDIATION
During severe weather emergencies (heavy monsoons, storms, or seismic tremors), Site Engineers form the immediate rapid-assessment team. If structural compromise or hazardous falling hazards are detected, the Site Engineer possesses statutory authority to cordon off affected wings, declare emergency closures, and mobilize emergency shoring teams.

1. PURPOSE AND ORGANIZATIONAL MANDATE
This operational charter defines professional duties, laboratory safety enforcement, equipment calibration schedules, preventive maintenance responsibilities, and reporting hierarchies for full-time Technical Assistants (Lab Technicians) assigned to engineering and physical science testing facilities across Demo University. Administered jointly by Campus Operations (DEPT-OPS) and respective Academic Engineering Departments.

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (TA)
Within the university nomenclature system, the abbreviation 'TA' corresponds to three distinct institutional concepts:
2.1 Technical Assistant (TA under DEPT-OPS / OPS-LAB-003): The full-time permanent technical staff member responsible for maintaining hardware laboratories, machine tooling, instrument calibrations, and student workshop safety;
2.2 Teaching Assistant (TA under DEPT-ACAD / ACAD-POL-007): The graduate M.Tech or Ph.D. scholar appointed to assist course professors with student tutorials, lab code grading, and remedial mentoring;
2.3 Travel Allowance (TA under DEPT-FIN / FIN-POL-006): The financial reimbursement per diem granted to faculty and students for authorized official travel and academic conference deputation.
- Disambiguation Note: Requests regarding machine repairs, workshop tools, component issuance, or lab technician signatures refer to the Technical Assistant (OPS-LAB-003).

3. CORE OPERATIONAL DUTIES OF TECHNICAL ASSISTANTS
3.1 Laboratory Readiness and Calibrations: Conducting pre-semester inspections, electrical machine testing, fluid mechanics sensor calibrations, surveying equipment servicing, and chemical reagent stock auditing before instructional classes begin;
3.2 Student Supervision and PPE Enforcement: Enforcing mandatory laboratory safety protocols (ACAD-LAB-001)—including the mandatory wearing of protective safety goggles, lab coats, and closed industrial footwear; operating primary emergency stop switches if hazards arise;
3.3 Component Issuance and Inventory Control: Managing daily stock registers for hardware components, microcontrollers, measurement probes, and mechanical tooling; collecting component return sign-offs before the conclusion of each lab period;
3.4 Equipment Damage and Breakage Logging (REL-018): If student equipment is accidentally shorted, dropped, or damaged, the Technical Assistant drafts a preliminary Equipment Incident Slip logging machine serial number, operating conditions, and student details for review by the Laboratory Faculty In-Charge.

4. COORDINATION WITH CAMPUS OPERATIONS AND IT SERVICES
Technical Assistants liaise directly with Estate Site Engineers (OPS-EST-004) for industrial three-phase power repairs, plumbing compressor hookups, and specialized ventilation maintenance. For computing hardware labs, Technical Assistants coordinate with IT Hardware Specialists under IT-STF-001.
